WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.064
Many C# validation failures begin as conversion assumptions.

2
00:00:04.064 --> 00:00:16.027
TryParse patterns for int, decimal, DateTime, DateTimeOffset, Guid, and enums give applications a way to handle invalid input without treating ordinary bad data as an exception path.

3
00:00:16.027 --> 00:00:19.324
A failed parse can become a clear validation error.

4
00:00:19.324 --> 00:00:22.698
A successful parse, however, is only the first step.

5
00:00:22.698 --> 00:00:28.296
The parsed value still needs range, context, and business checks before it is trusted.

6
00:00:28.296 --> 00:00:35.274
Culture-specific parsing matters because numbers and dates are represented differently across locales.

7
00:00:35.274 --> 00:00:40.795
A comma can be a thousands separator in one culture and a decimal separator in another.

8
00:00:40.795 --> 00:00:45.320
Date strings can swap month and day depending on format expectations.

9
00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:54.291
For APIs, invariant or explicitly documented formats are usually safer than accepting whatever the server culture happens to interpret.

10
00:00:54.291 --> 00:01:01.346
For user-facing forms, the application should be explicit about accepted formats and normalize carefully.

11
00:01:01.346 --> 00:01:03.570
Enums deserve special attention.

12
00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:12.159
Binding a numeric value into an enum can produce a value that is technically representable but not intended by the business workflow.

13
00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:17.450
Flags enums can also allow combinations that are invalid for a particular action.

14
00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:22.511
Validation should check that the enum value is defined and allowed in context.

15
00:01:22.511 --> 00:01:26.191
Treat enum parsing as the beginning of a rule, not the end.

16
00:01:26.191 --> 00:01:29.719
Overflow and range checks are part of safe conversion.

17
00:01:29.719 --> 00:01:34.166
A value can parse as a decimal and still be too large for a transaction.

18
00:01:34.166 --> 00:01:37.847
An integer can parse and still be outside a safe page size.

19
00:01:37.847 --> 00:01:42.218
A DateTimeOffset can parse and still be too far in the past or future.

20
00:01:42.218 --> 00:01:52.724
Safe parsing asks three questions: can the value be converted, is it within acceptable technical limits, and does it make sense for the operation being performed.
